Yes, that is correct, but does `await policy.CacheRequestAsync(context);` need an existing `IOutputCacheFeature` on the `HttpContext`?

Because if not the code could be writen like this, which means that the `IOutputCacheFeature` is only set when `context.EnableOutputCaching` is actually `true`.

```c#
var context = new OutputCacheContext(httpContext, _store, _options, _logger);

foreach (var policy in policies)
{
    await policy.CacheRequestAsync(context);
}

if (!context.EnableOutputCaching)
{
    // don't add IOutputCacheFeature when it isn't used
    await _next(httpContext);
    return;
}

AddOutputCacheFeature(context);
try
{
    ...
}
finally
{
    RemoveOutputCacheFeature(httpContext);
}
```
